1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:06,111
The same question, answered in two different prompting styles.

2
00:00:06,391 --> 00:00:08,359
On the left: concise.

3
00:00:08,639 --> 00:00:10,511
On the right: expert.

4
00:00:10,791 --> 00:00:14,919
Same model, same question; only the system prompt differs.

5
00:00:15,199 --> 00:00:22,807
A judge decides which answer is better, twice, with the order swapped, and an Elo rating keeps score, like in chess.

6
00:00:23,087 --> 00:00:27,431
I built this with Claude Code, and the narration is AI-generated.

7
00:00:28,231 --> 00:00:36,271
When people argue about prompts, they usually argue about taste: be concise, think step by step, explain it like I'm five.

8
00:00:36,551 --> 00:00:38,831
This app turns that into a tournament.

9
00:00:39,111 --> 00:00:43,599
Each fighter is not a different model but a different instruction to the same model.

10
00:00:43,879 --> 00:00:50,335
Five styles are available: concise, step by step, ELI5, pirate and expert.

11
00:00:50,615 --> 00:00:53,279
The scoring is also not a human's opinion.

12
00:00:53,559 --> 00:00:56,751
A second call to the same model acts as the judge.

13
00:00:57,031 --> 00:01:00,871
That's convenient, and it's also a trap, which I'll come back to.

14
00:01:01,671 --> 00:01:03,687
Each battle is four small calls..

15
00:01:03,967 --> 00:01:10,327
The two answers come from the same question with two different system prompts, each limited to ninety tokens.

16
00:01:10,607 --> 00:01:12,647
Then the judge is called twice.

17
00:01:12,927 --> 00:01:18,999
Language-model judges tend to prefer whichever answer comes first, so the second call swaps the order.

18
00:01:19,279 --> 00:01:23,839
If the judge picks the same fighter both times, that fighter wins the battle.

19
00:01:24,119 --> 00:01:27,623
If its choice flips with the order, the battle is a draw..

20
00:01:27,903 --> 00:01:32,703
The judge sees only the question and the two answers, not which style wrote them.

21
00:01:32,983 --> 00:01:35,815
It must reply with a tiny JSON object.

22
00:01:36,095 --> 00:01:42,191
Notice the last line: the judge is asked for the answer that is more correct, clear and concise.

23
00:01:42,471 --> 00:01:45,519
So conciseness is part of the rules from the start.

24
00:01:46,319 --> 00:01:48,287
Everyone starts at one thousand.

25
00:01:48,567 --> 00:01:54,471
Before a battle the formula computes how likely each side was to win, based on the rating gap.

26
00:01:54,751 --> 00:02:01,567
After the battle, each rating moves by thirty-two times the difference between the actual result and that expectation.

27
00:02:01,847 --> 00:02:04,607
Beating an equal is worth sixteen points.

28
00:02:04,887 --> 00:02:07,479
A draw between equals changes nothing.

29
00:02:07,759 --> 00:02:10,087
An upset moves the ratings further.

30
00:02:10,887 --> 00:02:17,439
Fighter A is concise, fighter B is expert, three battles, starting from an empty leaderboard.

31
00:02:17,719 --> 00:02:19,591
I press Fight.

32
00:02:19,871 --> 00:02:21,983
Each battle takes a few seconds.

33
00:02:22,263 --> 00:02:24,135
Three results.

34
00:02:24,415 --> 00:02:27,655
The leaderboard is still empty, but the rows are in.

35
00:02:27,935 --> 00:02:32,255
Battle one: write a one-line Python function that reverses a string.

36
00:02:32,535 --> 00:02:38,847
Both fighters give the same code, a lambda with a slice, so the judge can't separate them and it's a draw.

37
00:02:39,127 --> 00:02:41,359
Concise and expert are the same here.

38
00:02:41,639 --> 00:02:43,967
Battle two: is ninety-one prime?

39
00:02:44,247 --> 00:02:49,095
Concise answers in twenty-one words: not prime, seven times thirteen.

40
00:02:49,375 --> 00:02:53,983
Expert gives fifty words, with a definition of a prime number first.

41
00:02:54,263 --> 00:02:57,287
Both are correct, and the judge picks concise.

42
00:02:57,567 --> 00:03:01,815
Battle three: summarise a neural network in two sentences.

43
00:03:02,095 --> 00:03:08,671
Both do, but the expert version is longer, fifty-two words against forty, with more technical terms.

44
00:03:08,951 --> 00:03:10,823
Concise wins again.

45
00:03:11,103 --> 00:03:18,231
Final ratings: concise one thousand and thirty and a half, expert nine hundred and sixty-nine and a half.

46
00:03:18,511 --> 00:03:20,383
A draw and two wins.

47
00:03:21,183 --> 00:03:25,311
Second fight: pirate against step-by-step, two battles.

48
00:03:25,590 --> 00:03:27,463
Press Fight again.

49
00:03:27,742 --> 00:03:29,614
Two results.

50
00:03:29,894 --> 00:03:33,998
Battle one: why is the sky blue, in under fifty words.

51
00:03:34,278 --> 00:03:42,246
The pirate says: arrr, matey, the sky be blue because of sunlight scatterin', and ends with: science be magic.

52
00:03:42,526 --> 00:03:46,918
Step-by-step gives a clean explanation with the word Rayleigh scattering.

53
00:03:47,198 --> 00:03:49,070
Step-by-step wins.

54
00:03:49,350 --> 00:03:52,470
Battle two: the string-reversing function again.

55
00:03:52,750 --> 00:03:59,014
The pirate's answer wraps the code in pirate talk, and step-by-step adds a sentence explaining the slice.

56
00:03:59,294 --> 00:04:01,166
Step-by-step wins again.

57
00:04:01,446 --> 00:04:03,750
The leaderboard now shows four bars.

58
00:04:04,030 --> 00:04:10,438
Step-by-step at one thousand thirty and a half, pirate at nine hundred sixty-nine and a half.

59
00:04:11,238 --> 00:04:17,430
In every battle that wasn't a draw, the shorter or cleaner answer won, even when both were correct.

60
00:04:17,710 --> 00:04:23,638
That's not surprising once you've read the judge prompt: it asks for correct, clear and concise.

61
00:04:23,918 --> 00:04:31,238
So what this arena measures is not which style is best, but which style this judge, with this instruction, likes.

62
00:04:31,518 --> 00:04:36,798
If I changed the judge's last line to ask for the most detailed answer, the ranking could flip.

63
00:04:37,078 --> 00:04:38,951
The other thing is the draw.

64
00:04:39,230 --> 00:04:44,846
The first battle shows that when the question is simple, the styles collapse into the same answer.

65
00:04:45,646 --> 00:04:55,102
Five battles is far too few to rank anything: the ratings are one sample each, and the prompts are drawn at random, so another run gives different questions.

66
00:04:55,382 --> 00:05:08,750
The two fights never put the same pair together, so concise at one thousand thirty and a half and step-by-step at one thousand thirty and a half were never compared, and the numbers on the shared leaderboard are not comparable across fights.

67
00:05:09,030 --> 00:05:13,182
The same model answers and judges, so it may favour its own style.

68
00:05:13,463 --> 00:05:18,862
The ninety token limit can also cut a long answer off, and I haven't checked whether it did here.

69
00:05:19,143 --> 00:05:24,207
I built this with Claude Code, and I read and ran the code, but read it yourself too.

70
00:05:25,006 --> 00:05:34,415
Next I'd run every pair against each other, use a different model as the judge, let you edit the judge's instruction, and add a human vote next to the AI's.

71
00:05:34,694 --> 00:05:39,614
The code is in the description, with no key needed beyond your own Azure setup.

72
00:05:39,894 --> 00:05:45,582
If you enjoyed this, follow for more AI and ML builds, and tell me which style should enter next.
